// A shape set that cannot be closed.
//
// `deepen` builds a collection of its own type parameter, so it has to be
// handed a descriptor for `[T]`. It then calls itself with that collection,
// binding the callee's `T` to `[T]`, so that call needs `[[T]]`; and that call
// needs `[[[T]]]`. The set grows by one level per hop and never settles, so it
// is refused rather than chased.
//
// Whether a set settles is a question about the substitution a cycle composes,
// not about how big a shape is. Each call draws an edge from the callee's type
// parameter to the caller's, strict when the binding puts a type constructor
// around it. A cycle of plain edges is a renaming and cannot enlarge anything;
// a cycle holding one strict edge adds a level every lap. That is the occurs
// check, read across the call graph rather than within one term.
//
// So mutual recursion and ordinary recursion both close, and a merely deep
// shape is fine. Only this is refused.
//
// Erasure means this is one compiled body, and at run time it would merely
// recurse for ever the way any missing base case does. What cannot be written
// down is the descriptors, not the code.

fun deepen<T>(x: T): u32
  var l: [T] = []
  ret deepen(l)
end fun

fun main(): u32
  ret : u32 / 0
end fun

